This file is for those who plan to try and develop BrlTex.

BrlTex is a renderer for plasTeX, and does all its translation from the DOM created by it. All commands in LaTex are 
handled in functions in the renderer dictionary. Text processing is done by the textDefault function of the renderer, 
which currently passes the text on to translate.py. At the moment translate.py tries to do the translation of the text 
itself, but I think the current method may run into problems, so a complete rewrite of that file (or use an external 
translator for the text) is going to have to happen. Also something sensible has to be worked out for the default 
function, possibly it should insert a symbol to indicate unknown symbol, or it could prompt the user to insert a 
function, so build up a user dictionary as well.

To support multiple braille codes, you will need to create your own directory (to follow convention here use the code 
name) and then either use the files from the existing codes and alter those and store those altered files in your 
directory, or write your own. May change this in the future to be able to use one core renderer and have files of 
braille definitions (any ideas?).

Whilst you are free to do all that is stated in the license, I would encourage you to try and contribute back to the 
BrlTex project, so that multiple varients of this don't crop up with all their own strengths and weaknesses, but rather 
this project just gains the strengths of all the contributions and leads to a suitable end result sooner. My feelings 
are that this project first needs to concentrate on getting something that can be understood, then on the refinements of 
perfect braille output, rather than trying to get those exceptions but leaving whole sections unreadable.

